<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Leptis Magna</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 46 BCE (approx.) · capital Leptis Magna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Phoenician trading port on the Libyan coast that grew rich under Carthage and later became one of Rome's great African cities.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Leptis (Lpqy in Punic) was founded by Phoenician settlers at the mouth of the Wadi Lebda, east of modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a>, probably in the 7th century BCE. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Oea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabratha">Sabratha</a> it formed the three Emporia that later gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a> its name, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> shipping with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was governed by Punic magistrates (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffete">suffetes</a>) and a council, and Carthaginian Punic remained its official language long into Roman times. As part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> it was so wealthy that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says it paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> a talent a day in tribute. Its farmland produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and grain, and its merchants handled gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves from the desert trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a> seized the Emporia in the 160s BCE, and Leptis later allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>. Having backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s civil war, it was fined three million pounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> a year in 46 BCE and absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Roman Africa</a>. It later flourished as the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/leptis-magna.jpg" alt="Leptis Magna" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: joepyrek from Richmond, Va, USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leptis_Magna_(29)_(8288918733).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 46 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Leptis Magna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lepcis, Lpqy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=leptis-magna" aria-label="Leptis Magna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5047 992 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Leptis Magna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5367 1248L5259 1106M5367 1248L5166 1122M5367 1248L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0M5166 1122h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5367 1248h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5367 1248h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=leptis-magna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

